7=SNOWPACK 2 WEEK SUMMARY:
8=
9=Depth range over terrain: variability in HS has been reduced slightly since 33cm of storm snow Jan 30; 
10=however, this is hardly enough to blanket everything. Open ground, roots, rocks, are still visible or just under 
11=the surface.
12=Stability tests: Before the Jan 30 storm, stability tests were generally Hard. After the storm snow, field pits 
13=show the new snow is still reacting Easy to Moderate and in some cases very easy (fails with saw cut).
14=Structure: The extreme cold weather (-50) preceding the storm made a loose layer of facets that now 33cm of 
15=storm snow sits on. In some places this storm snow stayed unconsolidated and in others, a soft slab 
16=formed. This is generally the case in the alpine and cresting out of treeline. Below treeline is unconsolidated 
17=but a little more supportive than it was 3 weeks ago.
18=Snow temperatures: Mild temperatures (-2 to +1) is supporting the rounding process in the snowpack for now. 
19=
20=WEATHER 2 WEEK SUMMARY:
21=
22=HN/HST: A few days prior to the Jan 30 storm snow, an additional 10cm fell making the total snowfall over this 
23=period closer to 40cm in total. No significant snowfall since Jan 30.
24=Penetration: Storm snow has improved the supportive nature of the snowpack. BTL is only 20-30cm which 
25=before it was above your knees. Treeline and alpine, depending on where you are, ski penetration can be 
26=10-20cm or standing on top of a hard crust. 
27=Winds: Winds were L-S the day of the jan 30 storm. The following days it switched to Light N and NW. The last 
28=couple of days winds have L to M generally out of SW or NW. Feb 6 has the winds blowing M to S from the 
29=SW in the icefields area. This is depositing snow into the lower end of start zones. 
30=Temperatures: Temperatures were in the -40 range 3 days prior to the Jan 30 storm. Temperatures were -5 
31=max to -9 min during the storm. Since then, it has been reasonably warm days (-4) and cold nights (-21).
32=Settlement: The reasonably warm days since the storm has settled the snowpack. However, the weak facet 
33=layer which the storm snow fell on is still persisting in locations as settlement can be a long slow process.
34=Solar affect: A few days after the storm, many solar affected slopes at all elevations were releasing naturally. 
35=With this said, many slopes that were not solar impacted were also releasing naturally with the storm snow load.
36=
37=AVALANCHE OBSERVATIONS 2 WEEK SUMMARY: 
38=Prior to the storm, Na activity was hard to find. Immediately after the storm for 2 days, Na activity was very 
39=frequent especially where the snow load was enough the trigger the existing weakness in the snowpack or just 
40=from steep slopes unable to hold the snow. Eventually, Na activity has tappered off but many slopes that did 
41=not slide are highly suspect.
